 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 514 of the laws of 1983 amending the private housing\nfinance law and the real property tax law relating to the powers of the New\nYork city housing development corporation and the New York state housing\nfinance agency to finance certain multi-family housing; and to amend chapter\n396 of the laws of 1984, amending the private housing finance law and the real\nproperty tax law relating to the powers of the New York state housing finance\nagency to finance certain multi-family housing, in relation to the\neffectiveness of such chapters",
    "basePrintNo": "S6951",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6951-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6, Chap 514 of 1983; amd §7, Chap 396 of 1984",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Taxation",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6951\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 514 of the laws of 1983 amending the private\nhousing finance law and the real property tax law relating to the powers\nof the New York city housing development corporation and the New York\nstate housing finance agency to finance certain multi-family housing;\nand to amend chapter 396 of the laws of 1984, amending the private hous-\ning finance law and the real property tax law relating to the powers of\nthe New York state housing finance agency to finance certain multi-fami-\nly housing, in relation to the effectiveness of such chapters\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis proposed legislation would extend the sunset provision of subdivi-\nsion 23-c of Section 654 of Article 12 of the Private Housing Finance\nLaw, which authorizes the New York City Housing Development Corporation\n(\"HDC\") to assist in the financing of newly-constructed and rehabili-\ntated low- and moderate-income housing. The sunset provision currently\ncalls for subdivision 23-c to expire on July 23, 2021. This bill would\nkeep subdivision 23-c in full force and effect until July 23, 2023,\nthereby allowing HDC to continue to issue notes and bonds for the\nfinancing of housing developments.\n\n \nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nEnactment of this proposal would allow HDC to continue to assist in the\nfinancing of newly constructed and rehabilitated low- and moderate-in-\ncome housing in the City of New York. The bill would continue HDC's\nauthorization to enter into direct loan agreements with developers of\nlow- and moderate-income housing, as well as joint financing programs\nwith recognized lending institutions. Pursuant to a joint financing\nprogram, HDC lends funds obtained from the sale of its securities, both\ntax-exempt and taxable, to financial institutions, which in turn re-lend\nthe proceeds to private developments for the construction or rehabili-\ntation of low- or moderate-income housing. In cases of conduit lending,\nthe responsibilities of underwriting and servicing the loans are assumed\nby the lending institutions, which also bear the risks of default under\nthe mortgages. HDC does not rely upon the credit (or tax levy) of either\nthe City or the State of New York to pay bondholders.\n\nSince its creation in 1971, HDC has financed the construction or preser-\nvation of more than 275,000 units of affordable housing in New York\nCity. More than 210,000 units have been financed or refinanced since\n1984 pursuant to the passage of subdivision 23-c of Section 654. Over-\nall, HDC's portfolio includes a broad array of housing -from low-income\ntax credit developments, to mixed-income developments and aging and\nsupportive housing developments. The vast majority of units financed by\nHDC are income restricted to be affordable to families and individuals\nwho earn from 40% to 130% of the area median income.\n\nUnder the City's \"Housing New York\" plan, as of June 30, 2020, HDC has\nfinanced the construction or preservation of more than 69,150 affordable\napartments in less than six years. HDC financed over 76,000 affordable\napartments under the City's prior housing plan, far surpassing its\noriginal goals. HDC financing relating to the City's housing plan is\nprovided under a variety of new programs: (1) the Extremely Low and\nLow-Income Affordable program (\"ELLA\"), which provides apartments for\nlow-income people earning 60% of the Area Median Income (\"AMI\") or less,\nor if using income averaging for Low Income Housing Tax Credit (\"LIHTC\")\nproperties, 80% AMI or less, including deeply affordable apartments for\npeople earning no more than 30%, 40% and 50% of AMI or apartments for\nthe formerly homeless; (2) the Mixed-Middle Income program (\"M2\"), which\nprovides apartments for people at a mix of incomes, including low-income\npeople, moderate-income people earning 80-100% of AMI and middle-income\npeople earning 130-165% of AMI; (3) the \"Mix and Match\" program, which\nprovides apartments for people at a mix of incomes, including low-income\nand moderate- or middle-income people; (4) the \"50/30/20\" program, which\nprovides apartments for low- and moderate-income -people in projects\nwhere half the project is rented at the market rate; (5) the Mitchell-\nLama Reinvestment Program which anchors the affordability of the remain-\ning Mitchell-Lama apartments; an d (6) the Neighborhood Pillars program.\nIn addition, HDC has launched a series of other initiatives that use the\ncorporation's bonding authority, including programs using taxable bonds\nand recycled tax-exempt bonds.\n\nSince 2004, HDC has provided a voluntary Mitchell Lama restructuring\nprogram and a rehabilitation loan program for all Mitchell Lama projects\nin New York City. To date HDC has restructured over 50 Mitchell Lama\nprojects with approximately 38,000 units preserved and provided rehabil-\nitation loans for several additional projects with 5,000 units. HDC is\nstill actively working with Mitchell Lamas in New York City and expects\nto provide funds to preserve even more units over the next few years and\nprovide additional funds to extend the affordability period for develop-\nments already restructured.\n\nIn 2021, HDC expects to close on financing/refinancing transactions for\nover 40 projects involving the construction or preservation of approxi-\nmately 10,000 units of housing.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable consider-\nation of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
